      "message": "Let a quota say three different things\n\nA limit had two states where it needed three. \"The same as everyone\nelse\", \"no limit at all\" and \"this number\" are three different answers,\nand a field that can only be a number or absent makes the first two the\nsame — which is how every trap in this surface worked.\n\nSetting one owner's quota replaced the whole thing, so an operator\ntightening one limit granted unlimited everything else and was told\nnothing. A field nobody recognised deserialised to a quota that said\nnothing at all, so a misspelling removed every limit and answered 200.\nAnd zero meant no limit on the flags while meaning none allowed in the\nbody, so throttling a spammer with zeroes took every limit off them and\nprinted \"no limit\" as if that were the good news.\n\nWhat is stored for an owner is now only what was said about them, laid\nover the forge's numbers field by field. Absent follows the forge, null\nis no limit, a number is that number, and zero is zero everywhere: the\nflags and the command take a number or the word none. A field nobody\nknows is refused. The endpoint answers with what now holds, and reports\nwhat was said about that owner separately from what everyone gets, so a\nreader can see which of the three a number came from. The command no\nlonger merges over what happens to hold today, which used to freeze\nthis moment's defaults into a row an owner never escaped, and it\nrefuses a name that is not a person or an organisation rather than\nprinting a confident table about nobody.\n\nOne refusal is no longer remembered: a write refused for want of room,\nretried under the same idempotency key after somebody made room, used\nto be handed the old refusal for a day. Its cause was never the\ncaller's to fix.\n\nChange-Id: Isurface0105\nSigned-off-by: Mandip Adhikari <92096340+mandipadk@users.noreply.github.com>",
